WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.530
So we now focus on the Eulerian paths
problem

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:08.100
and try to learn more about de Bruijin
graphs.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:11.720
So, we saw that to reconstruct the genome,
we

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:15.240
need to find an Eulerian path in this
graph.

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:19.740
But some of you noticed that I cheated.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:21.920
I constructed the de Bruijn graph from

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:25.170
genome, but in real life, genome is
unknown.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:30.920
The only thing that is known is reads or
k-mers.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:33.937
Can we reconstruct the de Bruijn graph

00:00:33.937 --> 00:00:37.300
from genome composition rather than from
genome?

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:39.100
Well, let's try to do this.

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:41.880
But let's first review what we have done.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:47.590
What we have shown is that given a genome,
we can construct the de Brujn graph.

00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:51.595
What we want to do is, given genome
composition, or

00:00:51.595 --> 00:00:56.620
k-mer composition of the genome, we want
to construct the genome.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:03.240
What we will show now is that given k-mer
composition, we can construct

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:06.293
de Bruijn graph and then using Euler's

00:01:06.293 --> 00:01:10.810
insight to construct Eulerian paths in
this graph.

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:12.000
Let's start.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:17.810
So in the past, we started
from a path representing

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:20.600
the genome.
But now the genome is unknown.

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:24.090
What is known is only composition of the
genome

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:28.496
And we will represent this composition,
every element, every

00:01:28.496 --> 00:01:29.184
3-mer in the 

00:01:29.184 --> 00:01:30.002
composition by

00:01:30.002 --> 00:01:30.700
an edge.

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:36.010
We will now construct also nodes of this
graph by representing them as

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:40.730
2-mers as we did before. Now, to

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:45.720
construct the Bruijn graph from k-mer
composition, let's do exactly the same we

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:51.490
did before.
Let's just glue identically labeled nodes.

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:55.750
For example, AA and AA identically
labeled, let's glue them together.

00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:58.850
Bring them a little bit closer, and
finally glue.

00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:04.670
Let's continue with AT, the same thing we
see continue, continue,

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:11.320
continue, continue, continue, continue,
continue, continue again.

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:17.200
More, more and finally they're getting the
same path

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:21.330
that we were getting as if genome would be
known.

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:24.088
What we haven't done yet is gluing because

00:02:24.088 --> 00:02:27.400
they are, of course, other vertices to
glue.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:31.000
And we proceed exactly the same way as we
were proceeding in

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:37.710
the case when genome was known and finally
construct the de Brujin graph.

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:41.590
So what we have just shown that even in
the case

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:46.970
genome is unknown, we can construct the
same de Bruijin graph

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:51.050
as in the case the genome is known, and
this is great.

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:53.570
Which means that the next step we need to

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:57.360
address is how to find Eulerian paths in
the graph.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:02.510
So, for construction of de Bruijin graphs, the
algorithm is very simple.

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:07.250
We simply need to represent every K-mer as
an edge between

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:12.090
its prefix and its suffix, and afterward
glue all nodes with 

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:14.214
identical labels.
It is a 2-line 

00:03:14.214 --> 00:03:15.480
algorithm.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:19.185
If you implemented that you have already
figured out that there is a different

00:03:19.185 --> 00:03:23.490
way to look at de Bruijn graph. If you are
given the collection of k-mers,

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:30.880
we can form nodes of the graph from all (k-1)-mers
 from this collection, and

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:34.990
for each k-mer from the composition, we can
connect its

00:03:34.990 --> 00:03:38.370
prefix nodes with its suffix node by an
edge.

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:40.610
This is our two equivalent definitions

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:41.330
of the de Bruijin graphs.

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:45.950
And now let me explain what was de
Bruijn's contribution to

00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:50.280
this area and why these graphs are called
de Bruijn graphs.

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:53.970
De Bruijn graphs have great applications
of biology, in biology.

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:58.450
But de Bruijn of course did not know this,
because he was interested in purely

00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:05.920
mathematical abstract problems. He tried to
solve so-called universal string problem,

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:11.362
which is finding a circular string
containing each binary k-mer

00:04:11.362 --> 00:04:16.330
exactly once.
Here all eight binary k-mers.

00:04:16.330 --> 00:04:20.780
Can we find the strings that contains each
of them exactly once?

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:21.680
We can.

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:27.840
This is the string, and if you check for
each binary 3-mer, you will find it

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:32.250
appearing in the string along the circle
if you travel around the circle clockwise.

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:38.690
For example, for this 3-mer, it appears
along the string.

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:40.750
But how can we construct the string?

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:44.670
Is it even possible for all possible
values of k?

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:47.340
Well, the only thing we need to do, we
need

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:53.440
to represent each of these binary k-mers as
an edge.

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:53.730
Right?

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:58.050
Because it should be a k-mer composition
of the string we are trying to find.

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:02.220
Afterwards we need to construct the Bruijn
graph from this

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:05.860
isolated edges and then the Bruijn graph will
look like this.

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:10.500
And afterwards, simply look for Eurelian
cycles in this graph.

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:12.480
Cycles rather than paths because de

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:16.370
Bruijn was interested in finding circular
strings.

00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:19.900
Can you find an Eurelian cycle in this
graph?

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:23.210
And how can it help us to find universal
string?

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:23.470
Let's

00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:24.280
see.

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:26.920
Let's start working here, and we
immediately can

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:34.380
get insight into the first three elements
of the string.

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:38.620
Let's continue this way, and when we
travel through the graph, the string

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:41.160
keeps enlarging and enlarging and
enlarging,

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:43.800
and finally, we constructed the universal
string.

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:48.970
The fact that the graph is Eulerian implies
that every k-mer appears

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:54.500
exactly once in this circular string.
But what about this four

00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:58.360
universal string constructed from all
4-mers.

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:00.210
What about ten?

00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:05.240
All 10-mers in this case, the graph will
consist of thousands nodes.

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:10.100
Does it have a Eulerian cycle?
If yes, how can we find it?

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:12.810
We'll answer this question in the next
segment.

